WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.940
There’s a famous tale about a pumpkin-headed
demon that for many captures the spirit of

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.960
Halloween, Washington Irving’s Headless
Horseman.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:15.150
But I actually think there’s a much scarier
headless monster with the potential to keep

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.910
you up at night year round, one that began
in the folklore of my ancestors’—the Irish

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:22.160
Dullahan.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:30.500
[show intro]

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.960
Before we really get into the legend of the
Dullahan, let’s address the pumpkin in the

00:00:33.969 --> 00:00:36.449
room—the Headless Horseman of Sleepy Hollow.

00:00:36.449 --> 00:00:40.640
Washington Irving wrote the short story “The
Legend of Sleepy Hollow” in 1820.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.109
In it, Ichabod Crane and his rival Brom Bones,
both fall in love with Katrina Van Tassel

00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:48.930
because of her great beauty…and her large
inheritance.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:54.449
One night, after a rejection from Katrina,
a drunken Ichabod is chased down by a black-cloaked

00:00:54.449 --> 00:00:58.699
figure on horseback, and then mysteriously
vanishes, only his hat and broken pieces of

00:00:58.699 --> 00:00:59.699
a pumpkin left behind.

00:00:59.699 --> 00:01:04.500
With Ichabod out of the picture, Katrina marries
Brom and the townspeople are led to believe

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:10.729
Ichabod was carried away by a terrifying headless
horseman of supernatural speed and strength.

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:13.000
Most of us know the Headless Horseman through
children’s books or

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.700
the Disney version.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.580
There’s also that movie that’s loosely
based on the story, which has a lot of issues,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.500
including it’s demonization of women.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:23.590
Butttt let’s move on.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:27.820
I believe it’s not only possible, but likely
that Irving knew about the Irish Dullahan

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.509
before writing his famous spooky story.

00:01:30.509 --> 00:01:33.750
His mother and father were immigrants with
an English and Scottish heritage.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.490
He had a Scottish nanny.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.430
He travelled extensively across Europe.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:41.580
He was friends with noted author Sir Walter
Scott, who was indeed Scottish.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:45.340
So it’s hard not imagine that Irving was
exposed to Celtic culture.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:50.030
We could also give credit to Irving’s other
possible European influences like “Tam o’Shanter”

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:54.869
by Robert Burns and “The Wild Huntsman”
by Gottfried August Bürger, both poems that

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:57.030
have supernatural horse chases.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:01.630
Basically Irving took the concept of a headless
horseman from the Dullahan, and added elements

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:05.409
that would make it more frightening for his
target audience, Americans.

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:09.160
Irving uses the name of an actual town in
the United States and his villain is called

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:13.300
the “Galloping Hessian”— a reference
to the real German soldiers hired by the British

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:17.830
to fight in the Revolutionary War who were
known for their extreme violence.

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:22.920
So Irving takes fact and adds it to his fiction
saying “it is said by some to be the ghost

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.380
of a Hessian trooper, whose head had been
carried away by a cannon-ball, in some nameless

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.540
battle during the revolutionary war.”

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:34.030
The story of the Headless Horseman in popular
culture usually has horses, nighttime journeys,

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:37.850
carriages, and graveyards, all things that
we find in Dullahan legends.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:42.280
But, what’s unique about the Dullahan that’s
different from other monsters is that it doesn’t

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:43.280
actually kill you.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.400
You might get your eyes whipped out...because
that’s a thing they do, but your life isn’t

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.970
immediately in peril.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:51.580
What the Dullahan is though is a grave forewarning.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.640
If you seen one, it’s not good.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:59.270
You, or someone you love, will die, or suffer
in some terrible, horrible, eyeless way.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:03.970
The Dullahan story first appeared in writing
in the 19th century in collections of Irish

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:08.690
folklore like Fairy legends and traditions
of the south of Ireland by Thomas Crofton

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:12.680
Croker and Fairy and Folk Tales of the Irish
Peasantry by W.B.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:16.410
Yeats which introduced Irish legends to the
masses.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:20.850
The Dullahan can be either male or female,
always travels by horse, and is headless.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:23.830
Most of the time, their head is still with
them.

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:27.840
Sometimes it floats close to their bodies
or is tucked under their arm, or is even inside

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:28.840
their pocket.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.360
In many stories, the horse is headless, too.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.010
Dullahan can also travel in packs.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:37.130
In “The Harvest Dinner,” a carriage full
of headless passengers is driven by a headless

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:39.360
driver, and pulled by headless horses.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.890
Other accounts of the Dullahan show them in
the company of Banshees, racing human riders,

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:45.500
and even bowling with their own skulls.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.850
That would be the party of a lifetime.

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.860
In the aptly named “The Death Coach,”
a Dullahan wields a long whip and drives a

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.880
team of headless horses.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.390
His carriage wheels are made from human thigh
bones and the carriage is lit by two hanging

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:58.390
skulls.

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:02.760
In some versions, the whip they carry is even
more horrifying, it’s actually made from

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:03.780
a human spine.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.720
The Dullahan is the personification of death.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.780
The headless corpse was something the Irish
were all too familiar with.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:14.120
They were a predominantly Catholic culture,
with many stories and paintings that depict

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:19.400
martyred saints walking around carrying their
heads as a symbol of how they were executed.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:23.630
Even before Catholicism was introduced in
the 4th century, early Celts would take and

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:27.060
preserve the heads of slain enemies to dehumanize
them.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.310
They believed the head was where the soul
was located.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:32.100
So removing the head damages the spirit.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:37.000
Decapitation was practiced in Medieval Ireland—both
in battle and as a form of punishment.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.760
During late Tudor rule, the British government
even offered “head money,” to anyone who

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.870
could present the head of a known rebel to
the crown.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:48.030
There are written accounts of these beheadings,
and also archeological evidence.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:52.380
Across Ireland, both male and female skeletons
have been discovered with their heads forcibly

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:53.520
removed.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.210
This might explain tales of headless monsters,
but what about the headless horses?

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:02.150
Well, the Irish believed one of the greatest
insults was to bury a human with a dead animal.

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:07.250
And, in fact, many skeletons have been found
buried with dead animals with missing heads.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:11.130
So the Dullahan’s headless horse may have
come from this tradition.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:15.930
The Dullahan is a reminder to never “lose
one’s head” both literally and metaphorically.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:19.620
The appearance of a Dullahan often occurs
after the victim has succumbed to lustful

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.980
thoughts or has been drinking too heavily.

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:27.150
It serves as a midnight warning, that keeps
men and women at home, sober, and in their

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:28.150
own beds.

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:31.860
Oral folklore, religion, and literary history
all have a hand in creating this monster.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.150
One of the most terrifying things about the
Dullahan is that it closely resembles the

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:41.340
gruesome realities of Medieval Ireland and
combines fact and fiction, blurring real beheadings

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.830
with imaginary ones.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:45.000
And you thought the Headless Horseman was
just a Halloween story.

